WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.190 --> 00:00:07.300
So, you can actually cause the
phase behavior of a material

00:00:07.300 --> 00:00:09.670
to change by changing a pressure.

00:00:09.670 --> 00:00:10.930
Now, what is pressure?

00:00:10.930 --> 00:00:13.570
Pressure, of course, is
the effect of the gas

00:00:13.570 --> 00:00:17.110
that is around us, that is
squeezing on ourselves all the time.

00:00:17.110 --> 00:00:18.960
We get so used to it,
we don't even feel it.

00:00:18.960 --> 00:00:21.630
But actually, the pressure of
the air around us is pretty high.

00:00:21.630 --> 00:00:24.940
It's called 1 atmosphere of
pressure, and it's essentially

00:00:24.940 --> 00:00:28.210
a force that's squeezing
on us all the time,

00:00:28.210 --> 00:00:30.265
that our bodies are squeezing out at.

00:00:30.265 --> 00:00:33.100
Now, you know from either
experience, or not,

00:00:33.100 --> 00:00:37.900
that if you climb a very high mountain,
the amount of air that is around you

00:00:37.900 --> 00:00:38.740
decreases.

00:00:38.740 --> 00:00:42.100
So, let me show you what
the phase behavior of water

00:00:42.100 --> 00:00:43.840
looks like as a function of pressure.

00:00:43.840 --> 00:00:46.150
So, here's the phase diagram
that I showed you before.

00:00:46.150 --> 00:00:48.670
At zero degrees Celsius,
water transitions

00:00:48.670 --> 00:00:51.850
from a liquid to a solid, and
at 100 degrees Celsius, water

00:00:51.850 --> 00:00:54.370
transitions from a liquid to a gas.

00:00:54.370 --> 00:00:57.860
If we increase the pressure to
about 1 and 1/2 atmospheres,

00:00:57.860 --> 00:01:00.280
then it turns out that the
solid-liquid transition still

00:01:00.280 --> 00:01:03.500
occurs at around zero degrees
Celsius; it doesn't change very much.

00:01:03.500 --> 00:01:06.850
But the liquid-gas transition
occurs at a higher temperature--

00:01:06.850 --> 00:01:09.740
it's at about 112 degrees Celsius.

00:01:09.740 --> 00:01:12.010
Whereas, in turn, if we
lower the pressure-- so,

00:01:12.010 --> 00:01:16.180
if we went down to about, say,
3/4 of an atmosphere-- then,

00:01:16.180 --> 00:01:19.840
again, the liquid-solid
temperature stays about the same,

00:01:19.840 --> 00:01:24.340
but the liquid-gas transition
temperature decreases to about 92

00:01:24.340 --> 00:01:25.430
degrees Celsius.

00:01:25.430 --> 00:01:28.540
Scientists like to organize this in
what we call a two-dimensional phase

00:01:28.540 --> 00:01:29.420
diagram.

00:01:29.420 --> 00:01:33.130
So, this is a phase diagram where on
the x-axis, we have the temperature,

00:01:33.130 --> 00:01:35.470
and on the y-axis, we have the pressure.

00:01:35.470 --> 00:01:39.970
And what we do is we label the locus
of points where the material changes

00:01:39.970 --> 00:01:44.560
from a solid, to a liquid, to a gas, by
simply connecting the dots of the lines

00:01:44.560 --> 00:01:47.090
that we've just drawn before.

00:01:47.090 --> 00:01:49.220
And this is something that
we call a phase diagram.

00:01:49.220 --> 00:01:50.890
So this is a two-dimensional
phase diagram.

00:01:50.890 --> 00:01:52.723
And you see in this
phase diagram that there

00:01:52.723 --> 00:01:56.441
is a region-- there's a big region
where the material is solid,

00:01:56.441 --> 00:01:58.690
there's another region where
the material is a liquid,

00:01:58.690 --> 00:02:01.810
and there's a third region
where the material is a gas.

00:02:01.810 --> 00:02:05.320
At any point that you
are on the phase diagram,

00:02:05.320 --> 00:02:09.490
you can always find out what
the behavior of the material is.

00:02:09.490 --> 00:02:12.700
So, at 1 atmosphere, and
about 23 degrees Celsius,

00:02:12.700 --> 00:02:15.770
you can read off this plot
that water is a liquid.

00:02:15.770 --> 00:02:19.726
But this allows you to generalize
it to many other conditions.

00:02:19.726 --> 00:02:21.850
And so nitrogen has a phase
diagram that you'll see

00:02:21.850 --> 00:02:24.220
looks a lot like that of water.

00:02:24.220 --> 00:02:27.080
There is a solid phase, a
liquid phase, and a gas phase.

00:02:27.080 --> 00:02:30.160
At low enough pressures,
actually, nitrogen also

00:02:30.160 --> 00:02:33.700
has a situation where the solid
phase transitions directly to a gas

00:02:33.700 --> 00:02:36.220
without a liquid phase.

00:02:36.220 --> 00:02:40.090
But there's this big liquid phase
which is the part at 1 atmosphere that

00:02:40.090 --> 00:02:42.580
gives rise to liquid nitrogen.

00:02:42.580 --> 00:02:52.360
If we change the pressure, we can
change the phase of the liquid nitrogen

00:02:52.360 --> 00:02:53.770
by cooling it.

00:02:53.770 --> 00:02:56.660
I'm going to use this vacuum system.

00:02:56.660 --> 00:03:01.240
And as it evaporates, the
heat goes off with the gas,

00:03:01.240 --> 00:03:05.230
and the liquid that's left
behind gets colder and colder.

00:03:05.230 --> 00:03:12.130
And at some point, we're going to
get below the melting temperature

00:03:12.130 --> 00:03:20.090
of nitrogen, and hopefully
we will see solid nitrogen.

00:03:20.090 --> 00:03:26.010
First, I'm going to turn
on the pump, and then

00:03:26.010 --> 00:03:36.910
quickly-- So, the nitrogen is boiling
now, because the pressure over it

00:03:36.910 --> 00:03:38.580
is lower than its vapor pressure.

00:03:41.110 --> 00:03:43.900
It's internal heat that's
causing it to boil.

00:03:49.158 --> 00:03:57.440
It boils, it cools, and then,
what we see is solid nitrogen.

00:03:57.440 --> 00:03:58.460
Here is dry ice.

00:03:58.460 --> 00:04:02.060
If we raise the pressure,
we can put the dry ice--

00:04:02.060 --> 00:04:06.530
we can put the carbon dioxide in a
place where it can exist as a liquid.

00:04:06.530 --> 00:04:10.820
Going to seal it up so
that the gas cannot escape.

00:04:10.820 --> 00:04:13.780
Because the gas can't escape,
the pressure inside of the tube

00:04:13.780 --> 00:04:17.120
is going to go up.

00:04:17.120 --> 00:04:20.930
When the pressure inside of the
tube reaches about 5 atmospheres,

00:04:20.930 --> 00:04:25.880
the dry ice reaches a point
where instead of going directly

00:04:25.880 --> 00:04:34.310
to gas, subliming, it can melt. It
can turn from a solid to a liquid.

00:04:34.310 --> 00:04:37.610
Now, what's going on
right now is heat going

00:04:37.610 --> 00:04:42.830
into the tube is causing
the dry ice to sublime.

00:04:42.830 --> 00:04:48.260
As the gas builds up inside of
the tube, the pressure goes up.

00:04:48.260 --> 00:04:55.880
And as the pressure goes up-- there
it goes-- the dry ice now can melt.

00:04:55.880 --> 00:04:58.670
And we have mostly liquid.

00:04:58.670 --> 00:05:05.030
And if I release the pressure,
the liquid carbon dioxide

00:05:05.030 --> 00:05:06.960
can no longer exist as a liquid.

00:05:06.960 --> 00:05:15.430
And because it's still cold, it
condenses straight back into a solid.

